# Testing (Vitest)

- **Runner:** Use `npm run test:run` (non-watch) to verify the suite after substantive changes. When editing core label modules listed in `run-tests-core-label-modules.mdc`, treat a green run as part of “done.”
- **Scope:** **Unit tests only.** No browser-driven tests, no Playwright/Cypress, no end-to-end or “whole system” tests. No React Testing Library in a real DOM unless the project explicitly adds that later.
- **Unit of test:** A **unit of behavior**, not a single function or implementation detail. Tests should stay green when refactoring internals if **observable behavior** is unchanged.

## When to add or change tests

- **New behavior** → add a **new test first** (red–green–refactor): write a failing test that specifies the new obligation, then implement.
- **Implementation-only refactors** (same external behavior) → **do not** require new tests; existing tests should still pass.
- Touching core modules (`peptideMath.ts`, `LabelMathResolver.ts`, `LabelComposer.ts`, `LabelLayoutEngine.ts`, `labelModel.ts`, `Scaling.ts`) means **run** the suite; **update** tests when behavior or outputs change, not merely when code moves.

## Naming

- Use **`should`-style** descriptions so intent is obvious years later, e.g. `it("should print the draw volume on the label when the user enables print for that field", ...)`.
- Long, precise names are **preferred** over short vague ones.

## Assertions

- Prefer **explicit expectations on meaningful outcomes** (values, labels, safety limits). Avoid brittle ties to incidental formatting, log text, or private structure unless that structure is a deliberate public contract.
- **Characterization/golden files may use `toMatchSnapshot`.** A golden file (name ends `.golden.test.ts`) is exactly the "deliberate public contract" case above — the assertion is "identical to what shipped," not "equal to a value someone reasoned about," and hand-typed literals at that volume are unreliable rather than merely tedious. No other test file may use `toMatchSnapshot`.
- **`vitest -u` is never an acceptable way to clear a failure**, in a golden file or anywhere else — it is the "loosen assertions without fixing the product" shortcut below, just automated. A snapshot changes only after a deliberate decision that the new output is correct, with a note in the commit saying what changed and why.

## Humble Object: how we make untestable code testable

We do not test React components. That is only workable if components contain
nothing worth testing. So every decision must live outside the component.

A component (or hook, or any module touching the DOM, storage, network, or
canvas) must be **humble**: it wires together things that are already tested
and makes no decisions of its own.

Move out of the component, into a pure module with its own `*.test.ts`:

- **Domain rules and math** -> a domain module.
- **Derived / display values** (formatting, labels, enabled/disabled, warning
  text, which section shows) -> a pure `*ViewModel.ts` or presenter function
  that maps state to render props.
- **State transitions** -> a pure reducer. The component dispatches events; it
  does not compute the next state.
- **Orchestration of I/O** -> a use case that depends on **port interfaces**,
  tested against in-memory fakes (see `src/test/memoryLocalStorage.ts` for the
  existing pattern).
- **Browser APIs** (localStorage, IndexedDB, canvas, FileReader, download,
  scroll) -> an adapter behind a port. The adapter itself stays thin enough
  that reading it is sufficient review.

What may remain in a component: JSX structure, event handlers that only call a
dispatch or use case, `useState` for transient UI-only state (open/closed,
focus), and CSS class selection driven by an already-computed view model.

**The test for whether a component is humble enough:** if you cannot describe
a bug that could hide in it without also being caught by a pure unit test,
it is humble. If you find yourself wishing you could render it in a test,
that is the signal to extract, not the signal to add a DOM testing library.

Applies to new code and to any component you touch.

## Math and safety (`peptideMath`, `LabelMathResolver`)

- Treat calculator math as **safety-critical**: prefer **more** coverage here when in doubt, not less.
- Think **adversarially**: what change could subtly wrong-foot a user while still “looking” fine? Add tests for edge cases, boundaries, rounding, invalid/missing inputs, and **regressions for every bugfix** in this area.

## Unacceptable shortcuts

Never: skip/disable tests, delete tests to go green, loosen assertions without fixing the product, or use `@ts-expect-error` / `@ts-ignore` on tests to hide real type or logic problems.
